1. Amy Wu
title: Founder /Board Chair/ CEO
Emigrating to the United States in the 1990s as a first-generation Chinese immigrant, Amy Wu has navigated a multifaceted life journey—transitioning from a TV artist and singer to a wife, mother, entrepreneur, and dedicated community advocate. Through every stage of her personal and professional growth, she has remained grounded in a core belief: true beauty springs from health, genuine strength comes from within, and lasting happiness lies in balance.

Driven by a lifelong passion for service, Amy has long been an active leader in the Asian American community and a committed public servant, earning three U.S. President’s Volunteer Service Awards. Having witnessed countless women quietly balancing the demands of family, career, and self, she deeply understands that women are not only the heart of a happy home, but also a vital force shaping the next generation and strengthening the broader community. Inspired by this vision, she founded the Asian American Wellness and Beauty Association (AAWBA) to create an empowering space where women can connect, learn, grow, and uplift one another.

As Founder, Amy leads AAWBA with a mission to cultivate a trusted community centered on holistic health, timeless beauty, personal development, and public service. She firmly believes that when a woman nurtures a healthy body, a positive mindset, inner confidence, and a drive for lifelong learning, she naturally illuminates her family, inspires her children, and warms her community. Amy’s vision is to build an inclusive, sustainable platform where women discover their best selves, foster authentic friendships, and pass forward love and kindness to create a better society together.

2. Jiying Zhao
title: Founding Strategy & Launch Lead
Jiying built her academic foundation and professional experience at the University of Saskatchewan, Canada, and Northeastern University, USA. She has extensive experience in external affairs, strategic partnership development and sponsorship operations across multinational corporations, professional societies and international organizations. She has long specialized in cross-cultural outreach, membership program development and strategic collaboration across diverse cultural landscapes.
As an Asian immigrant woman, Jiying leads this nonprofit initiative as Founding Strategy & Launch Lead. Leveraging her expertise in alliance building, resource coordination and strategy execution, she works to cultivate a trusted community space for Asian women. She firmly believes health and beauty should not be confined to narrow external standards; they are fundamental rights that enable Asian women to center themselves, rebuild inner strength and grow freely. Her vision is to build an inclusive, sustainable support network that empowers more Asian women to nurture holistic well-being and radiate authentic inner confidence.

3. Robert Hao Wu, MD, FACP
title: Owner & Medical Director / Clinical Teaching Instructor

Dr. Robert Hao Wu built his academic foundation in medical sciences and traditional Chinese medicine in Guangzhou, China, and completed his internal medicine residency at Cornell Medical Center / New York Downtown Hospital in New York. With over three decades of clinical experience across major healthcare institutions, including Tufts Medical Center, Quincy Medical Center, and Carney Hospital, he is a board-certified internist and a Fellow of the American College of Physicians (FACP). He also serves as a Clinical Teaching Instructor at Boston University School of Medicine.

As a dedicated physician, leader, and active community advocate, Dr. Wu serves as the Owner and Medical Director of his primary care practice and Amee’s Medical Spa in Massachusetts. Beyond his clinical practice, he has held key leadership positions across various professional and cultural organizations, including serving as Chairman of the Association of Chinese American Physicians and the Gee Duck Sam Duck Association of Boston. Dr. Wu is deeply committed to delivering comprehensive primary care, advancing medical education, and fostering health, wellness, and community support for diverse populations.

4. Richard K. Chang, Esq. 
title: Board Director

Richard K. Chang, Esq. brings extensive experience in educational leadership, law, nonprofit governance and cross-cultural community engagement to the Board of Directors of the Asian American Wellness and Beauty Association. He has served as Head of School at Josiah Quincy Upper School in Boston and previously served as Interim Academic Superintendent for Boston Public Schools. Throughout his career, he has led major institutional initiatives, developed community and international partnerships, and advanced inclusive educational programs supporting multilingual learners and students with diverse needs.

Richard holds a Juris Doctor from Boston College Law School and an A.B. in East Asian Studies from Harvard College. He has served on the boards and advisory bodies of numerous education, youth-development and community organizations across Greater Boston. Fluent in Mandarin Chinese and shaped by extensive international experience, Richard brings valuable expertise in governance, education, community partnerships and culturally responsive leadership to AAWBA’s mission of advancing the health, well-being and quality of life of Asian American women and families.


5. Ruosha Pan 潘若莎

Board Director｜董事

English

Ruosha Pan has dedicated 30 years to researching and practicing education for children, adolescents, and families, and is the founder of Children's University in the United States. Over two decades ago, prior to the birth of her son, she pursued the study and research of early childhood education at Harvard University, aiming to raise the next generation with a more scientific approach.

Driven by her inability to find an ideal preschool, and determined to create a better environment for her son's growth, she stepped away from her former career and established an experimental preschool in the United States in 1999. Designing and building the campus facilities from the ground up, she pioneered an innovative early childhood education model based on cutting-edge international principles, integrating interdisciplinary research from neuroscience, psychology, and sociology. She personally developed the curriculum framework and brought together a team of master's and doctoral degree holders to manage and operate the school. Her journey of "founding a preschool for her son" became a widely celebrated story in the local community, ultimately benefiting countless children and families.

In her educational practice, Ms. Pan took the lead in naming her preschool "Children’s University," reflecting a vision that deeply respects children's agency and guides their holistic, long-term development. This forward-thinking concept anticipated what is now a globally recognized consensus in international education: early childhood education forms the essential foundation of human development.

Over the years, Ms. Pan has nurtured many well-rounded students. Beyond achieving outstanding academic success, her students have cultivated healthy personalities, positive life attitudes, a strong sense of social responsibility, and robust skills in collaboration and communication. Many have gone on to pursue higher education at world-renowned universities across North America and Europe.

As her educational practice deepened, Ms. Pan blended Eastern wisdom with Western educational science, drawing on the strengths of diverse educational philosophies. She gradually developed the systematic "Ruosha Pan Holistic Education & Four-Element Cycle" theory, continuously refining it through long-term practice. Her work has gained significant attention and adoption among schools, educators, and families alike.

Over the past decade, Ms. Pan has hosted numerous lectures, forums, seminars, summer camps, and online courses, empowering countless teachers, parents, and children to grow together and bringing positive transformations to tens of thousands of families. Her founded "Holistic Future Creators Academy" just held a science and arts summer camp for hundreds of children and teens in mid-August 2026. The camp featured full-time mentorship and inspiration from PhD scientists and artists guiding hands-on projects, teaching assistants from top universities such as UC, and over 100 dedicated educator volunteers—all working together in love to help children become navigators in the AI era.

中文
潘若莎研究与从事儿童、青少年及家庭教育三十年，是"美国儿童大学"的创始人。二十多年前，在迎接自己孩子出生之前，她便赴哈佛大学学习和研究儿童早期教育，希望以更加科学的理念培育下一代。

由于一直没有找到理想中的幼儿园，为了给儿子创造更好的成长环境，她毅然放下原有的工作，1999年在美国创办实验幼儿园，自建校舍，以国际前沿教育理念为基础，融合脑科学、心理学、社会学等多学科研究成果，探索创新的幼儿教育模式。她亲自设计课程体系，并聘请多位博士、硕士共同参与办学。这段"为了儿子创办幼儿园"的经历，也成为当地广为流传的一段佳话，并惠及众多社区儿童。

在教育实践中，潘若莎率先将"幼儿园"命名为"儿童大学"，希望以更加尊重儿童主体性的教育观，引导孩子实现全面而长远的发展。这一理念超前地体现了今天国际教育界广泛认同的观点——儿童早期教育，是人生发展的重要基础。

多年来，潘若莎培养了众多全面发展的孩子。他们不仅取得了优秀的学业成绩，也逐渐形成健全的人格、积极的人生态度、良好的社会责任感，以及合作与沟通能力，其中很多人进入北美及欧洲全球知名大学继续深造。

随着教育实践不断深入，潘若莎融合东方教育智慧与西方教育科学，吸收不同教育流派的优势，逐步构建起系统性的"潘若莎全人教育及四元周期"教育理论，并在长期实践中不断完善，受到许多学校、教育工作者和家庭的关注与应用。

过去十余年，潘若莎举办大量讲座、论坛、研讨会、夏令营及线上课程，持续帮助了无数教师、家长和孩子共同成长，为千万家庭带来积极的改变。她成立的"全人未来创造者学院"本周（2026年8月中旬）刚举办数百人参加的儿童及青少年科学艺术夏令营，由多位博士科学家及艺术家全天启迪、陪伴孩子，引导孩子动手做项目；有加州大学等名校的学生当助教；还有一百多位教育者志工协助；共同以爱心培育孩子，帮助他们成为在AI 时代的领航人。


